{"text": "RECEIVED Noy t 6 1978\n\nUNIVERSITY OF KENTUCKY\nLEXINGTON, KENTUCKY 40506\n\nSOLLEGE OF ARTS AND SCIENCES\n\nJanuary 31, 1978\n\nMe. W. G. Duncan Mr, Mcrae, Tr CRA|G\n5700 Coach Gate Wynde - Novis I1978 |\n\nLouisville, Kentucky 40207\nDear Bill,\n\n\u2018Thank you for your letter asking about nicotine addiction. I hope\nI can provide the information you need. Drug addiction is not my field :\nof research. Most of my information comes from the excellent book Licit\nand Illicit Drugs which Consumers Union published in 1972. This book is\nprimarily a compendium of the medical literature on drugs, but it was\nvery upsetting to a large portion of the membership of Consumers Union\nbecause the facts simply do not support the emotional prejudices our\nsystem of advertising and drug legislation have instilled into the average\nadult mind.\n\u2018The book points out that \u201ctobacco is one of the most physiologically\ndamaging substances used by man\" and quotes Dr. Vincent Dole, heroin re-\nsearcher at Rockefeller University as saying \"Cigarette smoking is unques-\ndonably more damaging to the human body than heroin\".\n\nAlso noted is that \u201csince nicotine is one of the most perniciously\naddicting drugs in common use, most tobacco users are \"hooked\" and, in\neffect, locked into the damaging effects of the tobacco\". Dr. Hamilton\nRussell of the Addiction Research Unit in London says \"If he smokes at all\nthe most stable well-adjusted person sooner or later becomes a regular\ndependent user (or misuser) - in other words, he is hooked\".\n\nPeople do not smoke for taste or flavor - these are simply terms\npopularized by the advertising agencies to lure the ignorant into drug\ndependency. The U.S. Public Health Service reported (June 1976) in their\nstudy Adult use of Tobacco that 61 percent of current smokers have, at\nleast once, made a serious attempt to stop smoking cigarettes entirely,\nand 9 out of 10 smokers have either tried to quit smoking or would\nprobably do so if there were an easy way to do it.\n\nThe CU book points out that when the supply of cigarettes is curtailed,\ncigarette smokers behave remarkably like heroin addicts. Following World\nWar II smokers in Germany under extreme conditions of nutrition, with food\nrations down to 900-1000 calories per day, would barter their food for\ntobacco. A major reason so many Americans are unaware of the addictiveness\n\nMIcROBIAL, CELLULAR AND MOLECULAR \u2014 eNVIRONMENTALRID AvdiGrionAny \u2014 REGULATORY AND DEVELOPMENTAL\n\nAN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY UNIVERSITY\n\nTIMN 368137",
